# Release Notes

## Unreleased

### New Features

- **Knowledge page: Review mode** — a guided, one-at-a-time triage flow over suggested knowledge candidates. Navigate with keyboard shortcuts (`P` promote · `M` add to merge · `D` dismiss · `→`/`Enter` skip · `Esc` close) and track progress via an "n of m" indicator. Accessible via **Start review** on the Suggested tab. (#787)

- **Multi-candidate knowledge merge** — an **Add to merge** basket lets you queue multiple suggestion cards (from the list or inside review mode), then promote them all into a single skill in one step. The resulting skill's Origin tab records every contributing lesson. (#787)

- **Skill rollout auto-reconcile** — the orchestrator now continuously detects team members who have been granted a skill but haven't received it yet (e.g. due to a prior sync failure) and re-syncs them automatically with exponential backoff and an attempt cap. The Rollout tab reflects live sync state, showing "syncing…" while in progress and surfacing the last error with a Retry button only after the loop reports a failure. (#787)

- **Stale candidate hygiene** — knowledge suggestions with no new supporting evidence for 4 weeks (configurable via `KNOWLEDGE_SUGGESTION_STALE_WEEKS`) are automatically moved to a `stale` state. Stale candidates are hidden from the default inbox view but accessible via a new **Stale** filter, can be manually restored, and are automatically revived if fresh evidence re-surfaces them. (#787)

- **Knowledge domain taxonomy** — teams now maintain a persistent list of knowledge domains (e.g. "Security", "Onboarding"), seeded automatically from the team's role templates. Operators can add, rename, merge, split, or remove domains via the new **Domains panel** in the Knowledge page, which also shows the current skill budget. Domain changes emit feed events for audit. Teams with no confirmed domains are unaffected. (#790)

- **Cluster routing against domains** — when a converged knowledge cluster matches an existing domain, it is now routed as an **amendment** to that domain's skill rather than spawning a new micro-skill. Clusters that don't match any domain accumulate until they meet a mass threshold, at which point a budget-aware **new-domain proposal** is surfaced for operator review. This significantly reduces skill sprawl. (#790)

- **LLM skill synthesis** — teams can opt in (`synthesizeSkills` curation config) to have SKILL.md content generated by the LLM in a structured playbook format (sections: *When to use / The flow / Rules / Gotchas / Examples*). When synthesis fails, a visible `[needs synthesis]` marker is written and the item is re-queued automatically. Off by default; the existing heuristic draft is written verbatim when disabled. (#790)

- **Skill distillation** — after a configurable number of amendments (default 5, `distillAfterAmendments`), a skill can be re-synthesized to deduplicate, restructure, and re-abstract its content while preserving all provenance entry IDs exactly. A **Distill** action with an old-vs-new diff modal is available in both the skill inspector (Knowledge page) and the Skills Registry. At autonomy level 3 distillation runs automatically; otherwise it surfaces a diff for review before applying. An unusable LLM result leaves the skill byte-identical — the skill is never corrupted on failure. (#796)

- **Consolidation migration** — existing micro-skills and open candidates can be migrated into consolidated domain-based playbooks in a resumable, per-domain process. Each step uses the existing merge machinery (atomic grant swap, lineage chaining, source archival), so grants are never briefly broken during migration. Remaining candidates after consolidation are automatically re-routed through the standard domain classifier. (#796)

- **Domain role surfacing** — skill cards now display the roles a domain applies to (e.g. "for: developer, reworker"), derived from the domain's role list. (#796)

- **Token usage: average cost per work item** — the Token Usage page's Work items tab now shows three summary cards — **Ø total / work item**, **Ø input / work item**, and **Ø output / work item** — so stakeholders can see the mean token cost of one work item at a glance without exporting data. Cards update automatically when the date range changes or the page is refreshed. Zero work items shows `—` rather than an error. (#797)

- **Team-scoped SSH keys and GitHub tokens** — credentials can now be owned by a specific team (`team` scope) or shared across teams by a System Admin (`system` scope). Team Admins gain a self-service credential management page where they can create and delete SSH keys and GitHub tokens for their own team without involving a System Admin. System Admins can additionally assign a system-scoped credential to any set of teams. The SSH Keys and GitHub Tokens pages are now accessible to Team Admins, and the Working Environment credential pickers show exactly the credentials a Team Admin is permitted to see. (#803)

- **Credential rename and reassignment** — System Admins can rename any credential and move it between teams or change its scope (team → system or vice versa) via a new **Edit** action on the credential table. Reassigning a credential that is referenced by an existing Working Environment is blocked with a clear error (409) if the move would leave the environment without access. (#803)

- **Team-scoped Model Providers** — the Model Providers page now follows the same scope model as SSH keys and GitHub tokens. Team Admins can self-serve model providers for their team; System Admins can register system-scoped providers and assign them to specific teams. Each team sees only the providers it has been given access to. Existing providers are normalized to team-scoped on read — no data migration required. The Model Providers nav entry is now accessible to Team Admins; System Admins continue to have full visibility. (#806)

- **Token Usage: per-team filter** — System Admins can now filter the Token Usage page (Summary and Work Items tabs, including CSV exports) by team. Selecting a specific team shows only that team's records; "All teams" remains the default and includes legacy entries with no team assigned. (#810)

- **Consolidation migration: operator UI** — the Knowledge page now includes a **Consolidation panel** that walks operators through folding accumulated micro-skills and open candidates into confirmed domain playbooks. The panel opens with a preview showing how many skills and candidates collapse per domain, then steps through each domain one at a time: **Review playbook** synthesises a draft for approval, **Accept & merge** applies it (archives all sources with full lineage, swaps grants atomically), and **Skip** defers a domain without applying changes. Migration state is resumable — reopening the panel continues from the first undecided domain. Remaining unmatched candidates are routed automatically at the end of the run. The panel updates live via the knowledge feed as merges complete. (#842)

- **Domains panel: AI-powered domain proposal** — a new **Propose with AI** action suggests 5–8 domain names and scopes derived from the team's lesson corpus; proposals are shown un-persisted for review and only applied on **Confirm**. When no LLM target is configured, a deterministic role-seed fallback is used and flagged with a visible warning. An over-budget nudge in both the domain list and the Add field steers operators toward merging existing domains before creating new ones. (#841)

- **Structured outcomes port for agent roles (data model / API)** — process template roles and live agents now support a structured `outcomes` field: a named decision tree where each option maps a condition to one or more actions (`setLabel`, `openPr`, `openIssue`). When authored, `outcomes` supersedes the flat `emits` list and generates the same graph edges plus an `outcome` id tag for traceability. Process templates round-trip `outcomes` through materialisation, member configs, and export/import (with `{{var}}` substitution in label names and conditions). `PUT /api/agents/:id` with `outcomes` persists it and clears any existing `emits`. UI editing and runtime prompt injection are deferred to a later phase. (#827)

### Bug Fixes

- **Auto-curated skills now show descriptions in the agent Skills section** — skills created by the knowledge curator were written without YAML frontmatter, causing agents to see only the skill name with no indication of what the skill covers. The curator now writes a `name` + `description` frontmatter block into every generated SKILL.md. Existing skills are backfilled automatically the next time they are amended. (#807)

- **Process Canvas: "Add agent" button now shows a busy state during provisioning** — clicking "Add agent" on a live team previously gave no visual feedback while the backend provisioned the Matrix user and seeded the workspace (which can take several seconds), making the UI appear frozen. The button now shows a spinner and "Adding…" label, dims, and disables double-submit for the full request duration. (#833)

- **Flow Editor: listener prompt now editable on existing label hand-off edges** — the *Listener prompt* field (the instruction the receiving agent wakes with) was shown when creating a label hand-off but was missing from the edit dialog, leaving it invisible and uneditable on existing edges. The edit dialog now pre-fills the stored prompt verbatim (or generates a default if none is stored) and persists any changes on save. (#834)

- **GitHub channel listeners no longer fire multiple times per event** — when two or more producers handed work to the same consumer over the same label (e.g. two Business Analysts routing to one Dispatcher via `dispatch`), the system generated one trigger per producer and the GitHub poller fired all of them on every matching event, causing the consumer to be woken once per producer. Derived channel triggers are now consolidated into a single listener per unique (events, labelFilter) combination; the contributing producers are recorded for provenance only and have no runtime effect. Existing teams migrate automatically on their next flow edit. (#826)

### Changes

- **Process Canvas emits now authored at the role/agent level only** — the per-trigger emits editor has been removed from the Triggers panel. Label hand-off edges are now defined exclusively on the role or agent, which was already the canonical location. Existing process graphs are unaffected: any legacy trigger-level emits are automatically lifted to the role level on import or materialisation. (#788)

- **Trigger cards no longer show the raw internal ID** — the grey monospace trigger ID line has been removed from each trigger card in the chat right-rail Triggers panel, reducing visual noise for non-technical readers. The ID is still visible in the trigger edit form, and HTTP-webhook cards continue to show their full webhook path. (#792)

- **Session Log: system event content now visible** — `system_event` rows in the Session Log panel (e.g. `thinking_tokens`) previously rendered as a bare label. They now show an inline summary of their payload (e.g. `thinking_tokens: 1234`) and are click-to-expand to display the full raw JSON — mirroring how other detailed log entries are rendered. No log data format changed; existing persisted logs show content on reload. (#821)

- **Token Usage → Work items: help text on each summary tile** — each of the 8 Work items summary tiles now shows a "?" InfoTip button that, when clicked, reveals a plain-English explanation of the metric. Covered terms include attributed vs. unattributed work items and what each count and cost figure represents. The InfoTip is also touch-reachable (tap to toggle). (#837)

- **Flow Editor: `duplicate-listener` lint warns when a manual trigger duplicates a derived channel listener** — the Flow Editor now surfaces a warning lint when an agent has a manually-authored `github-event` trigger whose event type and label filter are identical to a derived channel listener. This makes the overlap visible before materialising, prompting the operator to remove the redundant trigger. (#826)

### Breaking Changes

- **Token Usage page now restricted to System Admins** — the `/token-usage` route and all five usage API endpoints (`/api/usage/summary`, `/summary/export`, `/work-items`, `/work-items/export`, `/work-items/:owner/:repo/:number`) now return 403 for any caller who is not a System Admin or environment admin. The nav entry is hidden for Team Members and Team Admins. (#810)

- **Credential and Model Provider visibility now requires Team Admin role** — SSH keys, GitHub tokens, and Model Providers are no longer visible to plain Team Members in the Working Environment editor's credential pickers, or on the respective admin pages. Only Team Admins (and System Admins) can view and manage these resources. (#803, #806)

- **Removed trigger-level emits API endpoints** — `GET /api/agents/:id/triggers/:triggerId/emit-suggestions` and `PUT /api/agents/:id/triggers/:triggerId/emits` have been removed. Use the agent-level `emits` field (via `PUT /api/agents/:id`) instead. Any `emits` field supplied to `PUT /api/agents/:id/triggers/:triggerId` is silently stripped. (#788)
